Z||000001||Black88GT||09-23-2001||02:27 PM||||The Soviets only lost 15,000 troops in the campaign.  Compare that to estimates ranging from 20 to 40 million in World War 2.  I don't think it was much of a defeat.  I think they just did what we did in Vietnam, which is not fully commit to their mission, and eventually lose interest and give up.  In the Soviets case though they couldn't afford to keep it up like they thought.  Not to mention the fact that we funded the Afghans.  <P>We learned a lot from Vietnam.  And also, our troops didn't really know why they were fighting.  Now we have a cause, and the experience from Vietnam.  Ask WW2 veterans and they will tell you, Pearl Harbor was a driving force in the Pacific.  One vet said in a recent interview "It was always in the back of my mind". <P>They asked this question in the article "How do you battle a foe who has so little to protect in this world? A person who may believe a greater good will come from sacrificing himself, his home, his family? How do you vanquish an enemy for whom categories of defeat and victory, life and death do not match yours?"<P>Its easy. You kill them.<P>Also it says the Soviet vet says ""The Afghans will stop fighting each other and join together to fight you.."<P>Is that why the northern alliance bombed Kabul's airport the day of the attack?  Why did they already offer their assistance in helping?  <p>[This message has been edited by Black88GT (edited 09-23-2001).]||||reg||1
